	<!--
	  - The summary is the strongest place for the words people actually type, because the
	  - store search matches name, summary and description as substrings and every term of a
	  - multi word query has to appear somewhere. Measured on 2026-08-22: a search for "mcp"
	  - found this app first, "mcp server" did not find it at all, because the word server
	  - appeared nowhere, and "chatgpt" did not either. Both are true of this app and both are
	  - in the text now. The schema allows 128 characters here.
	  -
	  - "n8n" and "agents" joined them on 2026-09-04, and the reason is sharper than "more
	  - words is better". The ranking of the store search was read out of its source that
	  - day: nextcloudappstore/core/models.py, AppManager.search_relevant, builds six buckets
	  - in a fixed order, id exact, name exact, id, name, SUMMARY, description, and hands
	  - them back concatenated. A word in the summary therefore outranks the same word in the
	  - description by a whole bucket, whatever else is true. The n8n paragraph in the
	  - description below is the explanation and stays; this line is what makes the app
	  - findable at all.
	  -
	  - Two measured limits, so that nobody chases them later. A search for "n8n" cannot put
	  - this app first: n8n_sync carries the string in its ID and the id bucket sits two
	  - above the summary one, so place 2 of 2 is the ceiling. And "ai", "ki", "rag" and
	  - "workflow" are not winnable at all, because the match is a substring and not a word:
	  - "rag" answers with storageusage first, "ai" with rainloop.
	  -
	  - The second reader of this line is the app store inside a Nextcloud, and it is the one
	  - that installs. apps/appstore/src/composables/useFilteredApps.ts filters on id, name
	  - and summary only, so the description is not searched there at all, and it reads the
	  - summary of the instance language alone. That is why the German and the French line
	  - carry the words too instead of leaning on the English one.
	  -->
